The scoring, verified:

```css
/* (inline, ids, classes+attrs+pseudo-classes, elements+pseudo-elements) */
#nav .item            /* (0,1,1,0) — one ID beats everything below */
.list .item.active    /* (0,0,3,0) — three classes */
ul li.item            /* (0,0,1,2) — one class, two elements */
div a:hover           /* (0,0,1,2) — pseudo-class counts as a CLASS */

/* comparison is LEFT to RIGHT: (0,1,0,0) > (0,0,99,99) */

/* THE MODERN MANAGEMENT TOOLS */
@layer reset, base, components, utilities;   /* layer ORDER declared once */

@layer base {        /* base styles — lowest priority */
  .btn { padding: 8px 16px; }
}
@layer utilities {  /* utilities beat components BY LAYER, despite (0,0,1,0) */
  .p-4 { padding: 1rem; }
}
.btn { border: 1px; }  /* UNLAYERED — beats ALL layers above */

:where(.card) { margin: 0; }    /* specificity ZERO — always overridable */
:is(#a, .b) .c { }              /* KEEPS ID-level — the :is trap */
```

The cascade, in full order (what actually decides): (1) origin & importance — transitions > user !important > author !important > author > user > user-agent (browser default), and the NEW layer axis: unlayered beats layered within the same origin; (2) @layer order; (3) specificity; (4) order. Specificity is step THREE — which is why layer and importance disputes confuse candidates who learned 'specificity decides everything.'

The practice shift (how modern systems end specificity wars): utilities-in-top-layer (Tailwind v4's model) means .mt-4 wins over component styles WITHOUT specificity inflation; :where() wraps resets so user styles override them with ANY selector; and the escalation rule: never write !important first — it's a one-way door (only another !important with higher specificity/layer/later order beats it, and third-party stylesheets leave you no move).
